window.cncI18n.add({exact:E,rx:RX,html:[['#gvGuideMain',"\n<section id=\"gg-start\"><h2>Getting started</h2>\n<p>G-vis shows what a CNC program does. You type G-code in the editor on the left, and the view on the right draws the toolpath as you type. In 3D, G-vis machines a block of stock just like the machine would, with tools, chips, sound and physics.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Pick a program under <b>Examples</b>, or write your own code in the editor.</li>\n<li>Press <b>3D</b> at the top right of the view to see the stock.</li>\n<li>Press <b>▶ Cycle start</b> and watch the tool cut.</li>\n<li>Click a line of code, and the <b>Explain line</b> box tells you what every word on that line does.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"gg-tip\">Everything you type is saved in the browser automatically. Close the page and your code is still there next time.</p>\n</section>\n\n<section id=\"gg-skarm\"><h2>The screen</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Menu bar</b> at the top: File, Examples, View, Simulation, Tools, Courses, Lexicon and Help. On the far right you see the file name, the dialect, and the number of lines and moves.</li>\n<li><b>Playback bar</b>: Cycle start, Feed hold, Stop, Single block, the warning check ✓, speed, sound, dialect and home position.</li>\n<li><b>Left</b>: the editor and the Explain line box. Drag the divider between the code and the view to change the width. Double-click the divider to reset it.</li>\n<li><b>Middle</b>: the view in 2D or 3D, with the tool and position box at the top left and the camera buttons at the top right. The timeline is below the view.</li>\n<li><b>Right</b>: the inspector with the Warnings, Tools and Stock tabs, and program figures at the bottom.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n\n<section id=\"gg-kod\"><h2>Writing code</h2>\n<p>The editor colors the code: G codes, coordinates, feed rates and comments each get their own color. The toolpath redraws automatically, and <b>Ctrl+Enter</b> redraws it right away.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Explain line</b>: click a line or its line number. The box explains every G code, M code and address, for example where X and Y take the tool, and it works out the cutting speed when you program a spindle speed. <i>Read more in the lexicon</i> opens the right section.</li>\n<li><b>Warnings</b>: the green ✓ in the playback bar turns red if something in the code looks wrong. Click it, or look in the inspector's Warnings tab.</li>\n<li><b>Find and replace</b> above the editor searches the code only. Enter goes to the next match and Shift+Enter to the previous one. <b>Replace</b> changes the selected match and <b>Replace all</b> changes every match, for example g1 to G01. With <b>Whole words</b>, G1 does not match G10, G17 or G1.5. Ctrl+F and Ctrl+H jump there, and Ctrl+Z undoes.</li>\n<li><b>Comments</b> go in parentheses <code>(LIKE THIS)</code> or after a semicolon. The control ignores them.</li>\n<li><b>Subprograms</b>: <code>M98 P2000 L4</code> runs subprogram <code>O2000</code> four times. Write the subprogram after M30 and end it with <code>M99</code>. With Hass, <code>M97 P100</code> also works and jumps to line <code>N100</code>. The toolpath shows the program as it actually runs, and Explain line shows the line in the subprogram.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n\n<section id=\"gg-spela\"><h2>Running the program</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>▶ Cycle start</b> runs the program. <b>❚❚ Feed hold</b> stops it, and Cycle start continues from there. <b>■ Stop</b> goes back to the start.</li>\n<li><b>⏭ Single</b> runs one move at a time, like single block on a real machine.</li>\n<li><b>Speed</b> sets how fast playback runs, from 0.2× to 4×.</li>\n<li>Under <b>Simulation</b> you also find <b>M1 optional stop</b>. When it is on, the program stops at every M01.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n\n<section id=\"gg-tid\"><h2>The timeline</h2>\n<p>The timeline below the view splits the program into blocks, one for each tool change (M6/M06). With the ISO dialect it splits at <code>T… G17</code>. Each block shows the tool name and its time, and the width shows how long the block takes.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Click a block to jump to it.</li>\n<li>During playback the blocks fill up, and the current block has a white border.</li>\n<li>At the top right you see how far the program has run and the total cycle time.</li>\n<li>You can hide the timeline under <b>View → Show timeline</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n\n<section id=\"gg-vy\"><h2>The view and camera buttons</h2>\n<p>Drag with the left mouse button to rotate, scroll to zoom and drag with the right mouse button to pan. Double-click to fit the view to the toolpath.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>3D</b> shows the stock in 3D, and the tool removes material. <b>2D</b> shows the toolpath flat from above, with labels.</li>\n<li><b>Top</b> and <b>Front</b> turn the camera to a straight view.</li>\n<li><b>Rotate</b> turns the part over; see Turning the part.</li>\n<li><b>Section</b> cuts the stock in half so you can see pockets and holes in cross-section.</li>\n<li><b>Toolpath</b> hides the stock and shows only the toolpath: rapids as orange dashes, feed moves in green, arcs in blue, and a yellow dot where the tool is.</li>\n<li><b>Measure</b> measures distance, angle and radius on the part; see Measuring the part.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The box at the top left shows the tool, the line being run, the position, and S, F and T. In 2D, the <b>View</b> menu lets you show arc centers, G/M codes, X/Y/Z/I/J/R and F/S/T/H/D on the toolpath.</p>\n</section>\n\n<section id=\"gg-amne\"><h2>Stock, material and physics</h2>\n<p>Set up the stock in the inspector's <b>Stock</b> tab on the right.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Enter <b>X from/to, Y from/to, Z bottom and Z stock top</b> in program coordinates (G54), then press <b>Use this stock</b>. Z0 is normally the finished top face.</li>\n<li><b>Simpler</b>: enter length, width and height, or pick round, and click where work zero should be: one of the corners 1–4 or the center.</li>\n<li><b>Automatic size</b> fits the stock around the toolpath.</li>\n<li><b>Material</b>: aluminum, steel, stainless, cast iron, brass, copper, titanium and plastics. The stock takes the color of the material.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When you pick an example, the right stock and work zero are set automatically.</p>\n<h3>The physics</h3>\n<p>During playback G-vis calculates cutting force, torque, power and tool load, shown in the Physics box at the bottom right. Tool load shows how close the tool is to breaking.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If the tool breaks or the spindle runs out of power, the program stops and a crash box tells you why.</li>\n<li>You get warnings when the spindle speed is too high or too low, when the feed is so low that the tool rubs, when aluminum builds up on the cutting edge, and when plastic melts.</li>\n<li>Steel heats up and gets <b>temper colors</b>: straw, brown, purple and blue. Coolant (M08) cools it.</li>\n<li>Try <b>Examples → Physics → Physics demo in steel</b>, which shows all of this.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Physics can be turned off under Simulation. Under <b>Simulation → Stock resolution</b> you choose Normal, High or Ultra. Higher resolution gives sharper edges and clearer tool marks, but needs a faster computer.</p>\n</section>\n\n<section id=\"gg-rotera\"><h2>Turning the part (Rotate)</h2>\n<p>To machine another side, press <b>Rotate</b>. Three rings with graduations appear around the part: red about X, green about Y and blue about Z.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Drag a ring to turn the part about that axis. Hold Shift for 15° steps. G-vis turns the part in 90° steps, so the angle is rounded when you let go.</li>\n<li>You can also press −90 and +90 in the box.</li>\n<li>The rotation is written into the code on the line where the cursor is, for example <code>V X90 (VRID RUNT Y +90)</code>. Everything machined after that line ends up on the new side.</li>\n<li><b>Reset</b> removes all rotations from the code.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n\n<section id=\"gg-mat\"><h2>Measuring the part</h2>\n<p>Press <b>Measure</b> and pick a mode:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Distance</b>: click two points. You get the length and ΔX, ΔY and ΔZ. If the points are on two parallel edges, the perpendicular distance between the edges is also shown. The line turns green when it is straight along X, Y or Z.</li>\n<li><b>Angle</b>: click a point on the first side, then the corner, and last a point on the other side.</li>\n<li><b>Radius</b>: click three points along an arc or a hole. You get radius, diameter and center.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>With <b>Snap to edges</b> the point lands exactly on the nearest edge. A green dot means the point snaps to an edge. Dragging still rotates the view, and only a click adds a point. Esc clears the points.</p>\n</section>\n\n<section id=\"gg-verktyg\"><h2>The tool changer</h2>\n<p><b>Tools → Tool changer</b> (Ctrl+M) has three columns: tool types, pockets T1–T32, and details for the selected pocket.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Drag a tool from the list to a pocket. Drag between two pockets to swap their numbers. ✕ or Delete empties a pocket.</li>\n<li>In the details you choose the type and diameter (with −/+ or common sizes, for example the tap drill for M8), length, angle for chamfer mills and engraving tools, and name.</li>\n<li><b>Insert T# in the code</b> writes the tool change on the line where the cursor is.</li>\n<li>The inspector's <b>Tools</b> tab lists every tool the program uses and on which lines. A tool missing from the tool changer is marked red.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The Tools menu also has the <b>G/M code</b> and <b>cutting data</b> cheat sheets.</p>\n</section>\n\n<section id=\"gg-dialekt\"><h2>Dialects</h2>\n<p>The <b>Dialect</b> selector in the playback bar sets how the code is read: <b>Fanuc</b>, <b>Hass</b>, <b>LinuxCNC</b> or <b>ISO (DIN 66025)</b>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If you change the dialect with code in the editor, G-vis asks whether the code should be converted or only read differently.</li>\n<li><b>File → Convert to dialect</b> rewrites the program. Afterwards you see what changed and what to check by hand, and the conversion can be undone.</li>\n<li>In ISO a tool change is written <code>T1 G17</code>, blocks end with <code>*</code> and the program starts with <code>%NAME G71</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n\n<section id=\"gg-ova\"><h2>Examples, drawings and courses</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Examples</b>: ready programs in the categories Basics, Programs, Logo engraving and Physics. Stock, work zero and tools are set automatically.</li>\n<li><b>Examples → Practice drawings</b>: drawings where you write the G-code yourself. Each drawing has a solution.</li>\n<li><b>Courses</b>: twelve courses: first moves, arcs and circles, cutter compensation, drilling cycles, depth and Z, a real program, cutting data, pocketing, work offsets and G91, tapping, subprograms, and find the error.\n  <ul>\n  <li>Each lesson has a task, a hint and an answer.</li>\n  <li>The drawing shows as a light band in the 2D view. <b>Check</b> shows how well your path follows it and gives tips when something is wrong.</li>\n  <li>Your own program is put aside during the course and comes back when you leave it.</li>\n  </ul></li>\n<li><b>Assignments from your teacher</b>: open a <code>.gvisuppg</code> file with <b>File → Open</b>, or double-click it in the desktop app. The tasks work like a course. When you are done, press <b>Save answer …</b> and hand in the <code>.gvissvar</code> file to your teacher.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n\n<section id=\"gg-lexikon\"><h2>The lexicon</h2>\n<p>The <b>Lexicon</b> menu opens a window with tools, 3D simulation, cutting data, manual G-code, G codes, M codes, address letters, formulas and sources.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The window can be moved and resized. ↗ opens the lexicon in its own tab.</li>\n<li>The <b>Open in G-vis</b> buttons in the lexicon put the code straight into the editor.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n\n<section id=\"gg-spara\"><h2>Saving and opening</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The code is saved automatically in the browser as you type.</li>\n<li><b>File → Open</b> (Ctrl+O) opens a .gvis file or a G-code file from your computer.</li>\n<li><b>File → Save .gvis …</b> (Ctrl+Shift+S) saves a G-vis file. It holds the G-code plus the tool changer, the stock and the material, which come back when the file is opened again in G-vis.</li>\n<li><b>File → Save .nc …</b> (Ctrl+Alt+S) saves the G-code only, clean for a real machine and for other programs.</li>\n<li>In the desktop app, <b>double-clicking a .gvis file</b> opens it straight in G-vis, and Save writes back to the same file.</li>\n<li><b>File → Save</b> (Ctrl+S) saves to the open file in its own format. In Chrome and Edge, G-vis writes back to the same file without a new download. Other browsers download the file.</li>\n<li>The file name is on the far right of the menu bar. 📄 means G-vis can save straight to the file. A dot • means the code has changed since it was saved.</li>\n<li>If the screen saver starts while a file is open and changed, it is saved to the file first.</li>\n</ul>\n\n</section>\n\n<section id=\"gg-visa\"><h2>Display, sound and screen saver</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>☀ Light mode / ☾ Dark mode</b> before File switches the theme. The editor and the view keep their dark colors.</li>\n<li><b>🌐 Svenska / English</b> switches the language.</li>\n<li><b>View → Show inspector</b> and <b>Show timeline</b> show or hide those parts. <b>View → Full screen</b> (F11) fills the screen, and Esc goes back.</li>\n<li><b>Sound</b> in the playback bar: spindle, cutting sound by tool type, coolant and rapids. Set the volume with the slider next to it.</li>\n<li><b>View → Screen saver</b>: Off, or after 1–30 minutes. The mouse or a key closes it.</li>\n<li><b>View → Show intro at start</b> turns the intro on or off when G-vis opens.</li>\n<li><b>Home</b> in the playback bar is where the tool is when the program starts. Click the value to change it.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n\n<section id=\"gg-tangent\"><h2>Keyboard shortcuts</h2>\n<table class=\"gg-keys\">\n<tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>O</kbd></td><td>Open file</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>S</kbd></td><td>Save</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>S</kbd></td><td>Save .gvis</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>S</kbd></td><td>Save .nc</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd></td><td>Draw the G-code</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>M</kbd></td><td>Tool changer</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd></td><td>Find in code</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>H</kbd></td><td>Replace in code</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>F1</kbd></td><td>This guide</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>F11</kbd></td><td>Full screen</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Esc</kbd></td><td>Closes windows, full screen and measuring</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Double-click in the view</td><td>Fit the view to the toolpath</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Shift</kbd> + drag a ring</td><td>Turn in 15° steps</td></tr>\n</table>\n</section>\n\n<section id=\"gg-fel\"><h2>Troubleshooting</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>No toolpath shows.</b> Check that the code has moves (G0, G1, G2 or G3) with coordinates, and look in Warnings.</li>\n<li><b>Nothing is cut in 3D.</b> The tool must go below the top of the stock (Z below 0). Also check in the Stock tab that the stock is where the toolpath runs.</li>\n<li><b>A tool is missing.</b> The inspector's Tools tab shows which T numbers are missing from the tool changer.</li>\n<li><b>3D is slow.</b> Choose <b>Simulation → Stock resolution → Normal</b>, and lower the playback speed.</li>\n<li><b>The tool breaks.</b> Read the crash box: reduce the feed rate (F), the depth of cut or the width of cut, or use a shorter tool.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n\n"]]});
